[4.765, "o", "No \u2014 the tool says ASSIST ONLY, not ship.\r\n"]
[4.885, "o", "47/50 = 94.0%, and the 95% Wilson interval is [83.8%, 97.9%]. The verdict is measured on the interval's lower bound, which is 83.8% \u2014 well below your 95% target. So the point estimate is 1 point under target, but the honest read is that this sample can't distinguish a 94% router from an 84% one.\r\n"]
[5.005, "o", "Two things would change the answer:\r\n"]
[5.125, "o", "- More cases. n=50 is too thin to support a 95% claim. This run doesn't return a needed_n because there's no confidence signal to work with, but a 95% target with the lower bound above it needs a few hundred cases, not a few dozen.\r\n"]
[5.245, "o", "- Per-case confidence. Every case in this sample reported the same confidence, so there's no abstention threshold to derive. If the router emitted a real confidence per invoice, the tool could carve out a high-confidence subset to auto-route while sending the rest to a human \u2014 that's often what gets you to AUTOMATE WITH REVIEW at this n even when the raw pass rate doesn't.\r\n"]
[5.365, "o", "Practical read: use it as a draft-generator with human sign-off now, and re-run with a larger set plus confidence scores before putting it unattended in front of real invoices.\r\n"]
[5.485, "o", "If you have the per-case file, point me at it (JSONL with {id, passed, confidence}) and I'll re-run with the confidence path.\r\n"]
